course: dictionary
lesson: 5403
title: Kowtow: a deep bow, or bowing down too much
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from far away, and their story tells you what they mean. «"Kowtow" comes from Chinese for "knock head", a deep bow touching the forehead to the ground; in English it now means being too eager to obey.» So the first meaning is a real, deep bow.
explain: In a story, look at what the person does and why. «The clerk kowtows to the boss, agreeing with every silly idea.» Kowtows to means obeys too much and tries too hard to please, so the reader knows the clerk is not standing up for themselves.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Kneeling to the ground fits a royal bow, and trying too hard to please fits the clerk. The words around the word decide which meaning the writer means.
fact: «Kowtow» means to kneel and touch the ground with your forehead, to show deep respect.
fact: In history, people once kowtowed before the Emperor of China.
fact: In everyday talk, «to kowtow to someone» means to obey them too much and try too hard to please them.
fact: A «kowtow» (noun) is this deep kneeling bow.
fact: «Kowtow» comes from Chinese for «knock head».
exercise: «The villagers kowtowed before the Emperor.» What does kowtowed mean here? | Sang a song for the Emperor | Ran away fast from the palace | Ate a large meal together | *Knelt and touched the ground with their foreheads to show deep respect
exercise: Jo says, «Dad always kowtows to his boss, even when the ideas are bad.» What does kowtows to suggest? | Laughs loudly at the boss's jokes | *Obeys too much and tries too hard to please | Forgets to go to work | Stands up bravely to the boss
exercise: Where does the word «kowtow» come from? | *Chinese words meaning knock head | French words meaning to dance | Greek words meaning sea wave | Latin words meaning very old
test: «The servant kowtowed to the bully so he would not be teased.» What does this show about the servant? | He is laughing at a joke | *He is too eager to please and does not stand up for himself | He is climbing a tall tree | He is very brave and tells the bully to stop
test: What is a «kowtow» as a noun? | A type of warm hat | A short story about a dragon | A loud shout across a field | *A deep kneeling bow, touching the forehead to the ground
exam: Which sentence uses «kowtow» in the everyday, informal sense? | «The boat sailed across the calm lake.» | *«The shy boy kowtowed to the rich girl so she would like him.» | «The crowd kowtowed to the king in the old painting.» | «The baker kneaded the dough before dawn.»
